WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:04.120
Okay.

00:04.120 --> 00:10.160
So now I'm going to show you the coding order of a query compared to the execution order that happens

00:10.160 --> 00:11.000
in the database.

00:11.000 --> 00:14.280
So the coding order of a query starts always with a select.

00:14.280 --> 00:16.840
And then exactly after that you can put a distinct.

00:16.840 --> 00:19.120
And then after the distinct you can put a top.

00:19.120 --> 00:21.360
So this is the order of all those keywords.

00:21.360 --> 00:23.680
And then you can go and select like few columns.

00:23.680 --> 00:29.200
And after you specify the columns separated with a comma, you tell SQL from which table your data come

00:29.200 --> 00:30.880
from using the From clause.

00:30.920 --> 00:35.920
Now after that, if you want to filter the data before the aggregation, you can use the Where clause.

00:35.920 --> 00:38.600
And this always comes directly after the from.

00:38.600 --> 00:43.280
And if you want to group the data, then you have to do it after the where clause using the groupby

00:43.320 --> 00:46.040
and after the groupby comes the having.

00:46.040 --> 00:47.320
If you want to filter the data.

00:47.320 --> 00:52.080
And the last thing that you can specify in a query, it is always the order by.

00:52.080 --> 00:55.000
So this is the order of all those components of the query.

00:55.000 --> 00:58.520
And if you don't follow this order, you will get an error from the database.

00:58.520 --> 01:03.210
Now if you look to this query there are a lot of things that's going to filter your data, so let's

01:03.210 --> 01:04.170
check them one by one.

01:04.210 --> 01:06.650
The first thing that you can do is to filter the columns.

01:06.650 --> 01:11.370
If you don't want to see all the columns, you want to see only specific columns, you use the selects.

01:11.370 --> 01:12.730
And of course you must use it.

01:12.730 --> 01:15.850
So the columns that you specify will be shown in the results.

01:15.850 --> 01:17.450
So it's like filtering the columns.

01:17.490 --> 01:22.570
Now there is another type of filter where you filter out the duplicates if you want to see unique results.

01:22.570 --> 01:24.290
And that's using the Distincts.

01:24.290 --> 01:25.970
So this is another type of filter.

01:25.970 --> 01:30.290
Moving on, we can filter the result based on the row numbers.

01:30.290 --> 01:32.490
So we can limit the result using the top.

01:32.490 --> 01:35.250
But this type of filter doesn't need any conditions.

01:35.250 --> 01:38.210
It's purely based on the row number in the results.

01:38.250 --> 01:38.810
Now moving on.

01:38.810 --> 01:44.130
If you want to filter your data based on conditions based on your data, you can filter the rows before

01:44.130 --> 01:48.690
the aggregation using the Where clause and the last type of filtering.

01:48.690 --> 01:52.330
You can filter your rows after the aggregation using the having.

01:52.330 --> 01:57.610
So as you can see, we have like five different types on how to filter the results in SQL.

01:57.650 --> 02:02.530
So now let's see the execution order as we learned, the first thing that's going to happen is that

02:02.530 --> 02:05.250
SQL can execute the From clause.

02:05.250 --> 02:10.370
So SQL is going to go and find your data in the database where all the next steps can be based on this

02:10.370 --> 02:10.690
data.

02:10.730 --> 02:15.850
Now the next step that it's going to do is that it's going to go and filter the data using the Where

02:15.890 --> 02:16.450
clause.

02:16.450 --> 02:18.530
This has to be happen before anything else.

02:18.530 --> 02:22.050
So before any aggregations and so on we have to make scope of the data.

02:22.050 --> 02:25.850
So once SQL apply it maybe some of the rows is going to be removed.

02:25.850 --> 02:30.570
And once the data is filtered the third step is SQL can execute the groupby.

02:30.610 --> 02:36.730
So SQL is going to take the results and start combining the similar values in one row, and start aggregating

02:36.730 --> 02:39.650
the data based on the aggregate function that you have specified.

02:39.650 --> 02:43.490
So now after the groupby, after aggregating the data, what is SQL gonna do?

02:43.490 --> 02:46.690
Now we're going to go and apply the second type of filter the having.

02:46.690 --> 02:51.810
So based on the condition the SQL is going to go and start removing few aggregated data away and keep

02:51.810 --> 02:52.490
the rest.

02:52.530 --> 02:54.370
Now moving on to the step number five.

02:54.410 --> 02:58.210
Finally it's going to go and execute the select distinct.

02:58.210 --> 03:02.900
So SQL is going to go and start selecting the columns that we need to see in the results and remove

03:02.900 --> 03:03.660
the other stuff.

03:03.660 --> 03:08.300
And once the columns are selected, SQL is going to go and execute the order by.

03:08.340 --> 03:13.300
So it's going to start sorting the data based on the column that you have specified and the mechanism

03:13.300 --> 03:13.780
as well.

03:13.780 --> 03:15.660
So the data will be sorted differently.

03:15.660 --> 03:21.860
And my friends, the last tip that's going to happen in your query will be always the top statements.

03:21.860 --> 03:26.180
So based on the final final results SQL can go and execute the top.

03:26.180 --> 03:27.580
So here we are saying top two.

03:27.620 --> 03:31.660
That means we want to keep only the first two rows without any conditions.

03:31.660 --> 03:32.940
So SQL going to count okay.

03:32.980 --> 03:34.220
Row number one two.

03:34.260 --> 03:37.700
And after that it's going to make cuts and remove anything after that.

03:37.700 --> 03:40.300
So this is the last filter that's going to happen.

03:40.300 --> 03:41.540
And as well the last step.

03:41.540 --> 03:46.460
So now if you sit back and look at this the coding order is completely different than the execution

03:46.460 --> 03:46.900
order.

03:46.900 --> 03:49.500
In the coding we have first to specify the selects.

03:49.540 --> 03:53.180
Actually the select can be executed just almost at the end.

03:53.180 --> 03:54.660
So at the step number five.

03:54.660 --> 04:00.460
And once you understand how SQL execute your query you can understand how to build correct queries.
